<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#mawlid">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mawlid</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mawlid al-Nabi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mawlid is the celebration of the birthday of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, held by many Muslims on 12 Rabi al-Awwal, the third month of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>. Public celebrations are first recorded under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and those staged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori">Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a> in the early 13th century were famous. Some Muslim scholars have criticised the festival as an innovation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mawlid.jpg" alt="Mawlid" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Firdaus Latif · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maulidur_Rasul_(8413657269).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
